@startuml
title ThingsBoard release-3.6 - failure recovery and observability

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam defaultFontColor #111827
skinparam ArrowColor #111827
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #001F33
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #083B66
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #FFFFFF
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #9A3412
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #FFF7ED
skinparam NoteBorderColor #166534
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #F0FDF4
skinparam NoteFontColor #111827
skinparam DividerBorderColor #111827
skinparam DividerBackgroundColor #E5E7EB
skinparam BoxBorderColor #001F33

box "Durable queue boundary" #EFF6FF
queue "Queue provider\n(Kafka shown first)" as Broker
participant "TbKafkaConsumerTemplate" as Kafka
end box

box "Rule Engine consumer JVM" #F0FDF4
participant "TbRuleEngineQueue\nConsumerManager" as Manager
participant "SubmitStrategy" as Submit
participant "TbMsgPackProcessingContext" as Pack
participant "TbRuleEngineProcessing\nStrategy" as Strategy
end box

box "Actor and side effects" #FFF7ED
participant "Tenant / RuleChain /\nRuleNode actors" as Actors
participant "Rule Node" as Node
database "PostgreSQL / external target" as Target
end box

== Successful processing and explicit commit boundary ==

Manager -> Kafka: poll(queue.pollInterval)
Kafka -> Broker: KafkaConsumer.poll(duration)
Broker --> Kafka: records at current position
Kafka -> Kafka: decode each record
Kafka --> Manager: List<TbProtoQueueMsg>
Manager -> Submit: init(msgs)
Manager -> Pack: new(pack, skipTimeoutPossible)
Manager -> Submit: submitAttempt(callback)
Submit -> Manager: submitMessage(id, msg)
Manager -> Pack: create TbMsgPackCallback
Manager -> Actors: ActorSystemContext.tell(QueueToRuleEngineMsg)
Actors -> Node: tbNode.onMsg(...)
Node -> Target: business side effect
Target --> Node: commit / external response
Node -> Pack: callback.onSuccess()
Pack -> Pack: pending -> success; latch countDown
Manager -> Pack: await(packProcessingTimeout)
Pack --> Manager: completed
Manager -> Strategy: analyze(result)
Strategy --> Manager: decision(commit=true)
Manager -> Pack: cleanup(); clear maps; canceled=true
note right of Pack
isCanceled = skipTimeoutMsgsPossible AND canceled.
Default Main SKIP_ALL_FAILURES passes false, so its late
callback remains valid and Rule Node work may continue after commit.
Retry strategies and SKIP_ALL_FAILURES_AND_TIMED_OUT pass true.
end note
Manager -> Submit: stop()
Manager -> Kafka: commit()
Kafka -> Broker: commitSync()
alt offset commit acknowledged
  Broker --> Kafka: success
  Kafka --> Manager: return
else commitSync throws
  Broker --> Kafka: commit exception
  Kafka --> Manager: exception
  Manager -> Manager: outer catch; warn; sleep(pollDuration)
  Manager -> Kafka: next poll() without seek or pack resubmit
  note over Manager,Broker
  Consumer position is already after the failed pack.
  A later higher-offset commit may cross it; restart/rebalance before
  that commit may instead redeliver from the old committed offset.
  end note
end

note over Target,Broker
The database/external effect and queue offset are not one transaction.
Success followed by process death before commit allows redelivery.
Idempotency belongs to the Rule Node and target data model.
end note

== Failure, timeout, in-memory reprocess, and final skip ==

Manager -> Kafka: poll(pack)
Kafka --> Manager: records
Manager -> Pack: pendingMap = records
Manager -> Actors: submit all / strategy-selected records

alt callback failure
  Actors -> Pack: onFailure(tenantId, id, exception)
  Pack -> Pack: pending -> failed
else processing timeout
  Manager -> Pack: await(...) returns false
  note right of Pack
  Timeout does not cancel Actor or Rule Node work.
  Late callbacks can arrive after cleanup. They are invalid only when
  skipTimeoutMsgsPossible=true; default Main passes false.
  end note
else rate limit exception
  Actors -> Pack: TbMsgPackCallback.onRateLimit(...)
  Pack -> Pack: onSuccess(id)
end

Manager -> Strategy: analyze(pending/failed/success)
alt retry selected records
  Strategy -> Strategy: retryCount++
  note right of Strategy
  Default High Priority: RETRY_FAILED_AND_TIMED_OUT,
  retries=0 and failurePercentage=0, so no retry-count limit.
  Default Sequential By Originator: retries=3.
  end note
  Strategy -> Strategy: sleep(pauseBetweenRetries)
  Strategy --> Manager: commit=false + reprocessMap
  Manager -> Pack: cleanup old context; validity is strategy-dependent
  Manager -> Submit: update(reprocessMap)
  Manager -> Pack: new context
  Manager -> Actors: resubmit selected records
else max retries / failure percentage / skip strategy
  Strategy --> Manager: commit=true
  Manager -> Kafka: commitSync()
  note over Manager,Broker
  No application DLQ write occurs here.
  Remaining failed or timed-out payloads are not durably recorded as attempts.
  end note
end

== Process crash windows ==

group Crash A: after side effect, before broker commit
  Node -> Target: commit side effect
  Target --> Node: success
  Node -> Pack: callback success
  Manager -x Kafka: process dies before commit
  Broker -> Kafka: redeliver from committed offset
  Kafka -> Manager: same record after restart
  Manager -> Actors: execute again
end

group Crash B: after poll, before side effect
  Broker -> Kafka: record
  Kafka --> Manager: decoded record
  Manager -x Actors: process dies
  Broker -> Kafka: redeliver if provider retained unconfirmed record
end

group Decode poison
  Broker -> Kafka: records N, N+1, N+2
  Kafka -> Kafka: decode N succeeds
  Kafka -> Kafka: decode N+1 throws IOException
  Kafka --> Manager: poll throws RuntimeException
  Manager -> Manager: warn + sleep pollDuration
  note over Kafka,Broker
  There is no common seek, nack, or DLQ call.
  Kafka position and committed offset now differ;
  later commit or restart/rebalance determines the observed outcome.
  end note
end

newpage Provider commit methods are not equivalent

participant "TB consumer caller" as Caller
participant "Provider template" as Provider
queue "Remote provider" as Remote
participant "Local async executor" as LocalExec

== Kafka ==
Caller -> Provider: commit()
Provider -> Remote: Kafka commitSync()
Remote --> Provider: success or exception
Provider --> Caller: return or throw

== RabbitMQ ==
Provider -> Remote: queueDeclare(durable=false, exclusive=false, autoDelete=false)
Provider -> Remote: basicPublish(empty BasicProperties)
note over Provider,Remote
The queue is non-durable and the message is non-persistent.
The producer does not enable publisher confirms.
end note
Caller -> Provider: commit()
Provider -> Remote: basicAck(0, true)
alt IOException
  Remote --> Provider: IOException
  Provider -> Provider: log.error; do not rethrow
end
Provider --> Caller: return
note over Caller,Remote
basicAck directly sends a method frame; its return is not a broker
acknowledgement. A normal return therefore does not prove durable confirm.
end note

== AWS SQS ==
Caller -> Provider: commit()
Provider -> LocalExec: submit(deleteMessageBatch)
Provider -> Provider: pendingMessages.clear()
Provider --> Caller: return before delete result
LocalExec -> Remote: deleteMessageBatch later
Remote --> LocalExec: success/failure not propagated to caller

== Google Pub/Sub ==
Caller -> Provider: commit()
Provider -> Remote: acknowledgeCallable().futureCall()
Provider -> Provider: acknowledgeRequests.clear()
Provider --> Caller: return before future result

== Azure Service Bus ==
Caller -> Provider: commit()
Provider -> Remote: completeMessageAsync(deliveryTag)
Provider -> Provider: pendingMessages.clear()
Provider --> Caller: return before completion result

== In-memory ==
Caller -> Provider: poll() removes from JVM storage
Caller -> Provider: commit()
Provider --> Caller: no-op

note over Caller,Remote
The common commit() name does not make remote acknowledgement synchronous.
Provider redelivery, visibility timeout, ack deadline, and channel state remain provider-specific.
end note

newpage Actor initialization, process failure, and poison behavior

box "Actor system JVM" #EFF6FF
participant "DefaultTbActorSystem" as ActorSystem
participant "TbActorMailbox" as Mailbox
participant "TbActor" as Actor
participant "ComponentMsgProcessor" as Processor
end box
participant "Rule Engine pack callback" as ReCallback

== Initialization retry ==
ActorSystem -> Mailbox: createActor(); initActor()
Mailbox -> Actor: init(ctx), attempt=1
alt init succeeds
  Actor --> Mailbox: return
  Mailbox -> Mailbox: ready=true; process queues
else recoverable init failure
  Actor --> Mailbox: Throwable
  Mailbox -> Actor: onInitFailure(attempt, t)
  Actor --> Mailbox: retryWithDelay(5000 * attempt)
  Mailbox -> Mailbox: schedule next tryInit
else unrecoverable or maximum attempts
  Mailbox -> Mailbox: stopReason=INIT_FAILED
  Mailbox -> Actor: destroy(INIT_FAILED, cause)
  Mailbox -> ReCallback: onTbActorStopped for queued messages
end

== Process failure is resume/stop, not retry/restart ==
Mailbox -> Mailbox: poll one mailbox message
Mailbox -> Actor: process(msg)
alt ordinary Exception escapes
  Actor --> Mailbox: Exception
  Mailbox -> Actor: onProcessFailure(msg, t)
  Actor --> Mailbox: ProcessFailureStrategy.resume()
  Mailbox -> Mailbox: continue with next message
  note right of Mailbox
  The current message is not put back into the mailbox.
  end note
else Error escapes
  Actor --> Mailbox: Error
  Actor --> Mailbox: ProcessFailureStrategy.stop()
  Mailbox -> ActorSystem: stop(selfId)
  ActorSystem -> Actor: destroy(STOPPED, cause)
else TbRuleNodeUpdateException
  Actor --> Mailbox: update exception
  Mailbox -> Actor: destroy(INIT_FAILED, cause)
end

== Component lifecycle restart ==
Actor -> Processor: onUpdate(ctx) / onActivate(ctx)
Processor -> Processor: stop(ctx)
Processor -> Processor: start(ctx)
note right of Processor
This restart is processor lifecycle handling.
It is not automatic replay of the message that triggered a failure.
end note

== Rule Node ordinary exception ==
Mailbox -> Actor: RuleNodeActor.process(msg)
Actor -> Processor: onRuleChainToRuleNodeMsg
Processor -> Processor: tbNode.onMsg
alt node throws Exception
  Processor -> Processor: persist debug/error where enabled
  Processor -> ReCallback: route Failure or onFailure
end

newpage Transport sessions, service restart, and subscription repair

box "Transport process" #F0FDF4
participant "Protocol handler" as Protocol
participant "DefaultTransportService" as Transport
collections "sessions<UUID, SessionMetaData>" as LocalSessions
end box

queue "Core queue" as CoreQueue

box "Core process" #EFF6FF
participant "DeviceActor" as DeviceActor
collections "DeviceActor session maps" as ActorSessions
participant "TbCoreStartupService" as Startup
participant "LocalSubscriptionService" as LocalSubs
participant "DefaultSubscription\nManagerService" as SubManager
participant "DefaultActorService" as ActorService
participant "HashPartitionService" as PartitionService
end box

database "Shared cache (Redis mode)" as Cache
participant "ZkDiscoveryService" as Zk
participant "Device client" as Device

== Session open and snapshot ==
Device -> Protocol: TCP/MQTT connect
Protocol -> Transport: registerAsyncSession(info, listener)
Transport -> LocalSessions: computeIfAbsent(sessionId, listener)
Protocol -> Transport: process(OPEN / subscribe)
Transport -> CoreQueue: ToDeviceActorMsg
CoreQueue -> DeviceActor: process session message
DeviceActor -> ActorSessions: add ASYNC session/subscriptions
DeviceActor -> Cache: dumpSessions() [non-local cache only]

== Transport process death ==
Transport -x LocalSessions: JVM dies; listeners lost
Protocol -x Device: socket closes
Zk -> SubManager: OtherServiceShutdownEvent(serviceId, serviceTypes)
SubManager -> SubManager: remove subscription ownership for stopped Core
Zk -> PartitionService: delayed recalculatePartitions(...)
PartitionService -> ActorService: PartitionChangeEvent (separate event path)
ActorService -> DeviceActor: PartitionChangeMsg via AppActor/TenantActor tree
DeviceActor -> DeviceActor: stop if TB_CORE partition moved
note over LocalSessions,Cache
The shared cache contains metadata, nodeId, activity, and subscription flags.
It does not contain a live socket or SessionMsgListener.
SYNC sessions are excluded from the snapshot.
end note
note over Zk,DeviceActor
OtherServiceShutdownEvent is not sent directly to DeviceActor.
Subscription ownership cleanup and Actor partition adjustment are distinct flows.
end note

== DeviceActor recreation ==
DeviceActor -> Cache: restoreSessions()
Cache --> DeviceActor: ASYNC metadata snapshot or decode failure
DeviceActor -> ActorSessions: rebuild metadata maps
DeviceActor -> Transport: downlink to old nodeId/sessionId
Transport -> LocalSessions: sessions.get(sessionId)
alt no live local session
  LocalSessions --> Transport: null
  Transport -> Transport: no listener delivery
end

== Real connection recovery ==
Device -> Protocol: client reconnect/backoff
Protocol -> Transport: new register + OPEN + subscriptions
Transport -> CoreQueue: new session state
CoreQueue -> DeviceActor: replace/update snapshot

== Core startup subscription repair ==
Startup -> CoreQueue: broadcast CoreStartupMsg(serviceId, partitions)
CoreQueue -> LocalSubs: onCoreStartupMsg(msg)
LocalSubs -> LocalSubs: submit asynchronous repair task
LocalSubs --> CoreQueue: callback success before repair completes
LocalSubs -> CoreQueue: push UPDATED subscriptions to new Core partitions

note over Startup,LocalSubs
CoreStartupMsg repairs telemetry/WebSocket subscription ownership only.
It is not a barrier for all service state and does not restore transport connections.
end note

newpage Database queues, futures, and observability

box "PostgreSQL async batch" #FFF7ED
participant "DAO caller" as DaoCaller
queue "TbSqlBlockingQueue" as SqlQueue
participant "Batch repository" as Repository
database "PostgreSQL" as Pg
end box

box "Cassandra buffered executor" #EFF6FF
participant "AbstractBufferedRateExecutor" as CassExec
participant "Cassandra driver future" as Driver
database "Cassandra" as Cassandra
end box

box "Signals" #F0FDF4
participant "DefaultStatsFactory" as Stats
participant "Periodic loggers" as Logs
participant "Actuator / Prometheus" as Prom
participant "KafkaConsumerStatsService" as Lag
participant "Debug Event / Audit" as Events
end box

== PostgreSQL batch future ==
DaoCaller -> SqlQueue: add(entity)
SqlQueue --> DaoCaller: pending SettableFuture
SqlQueue -> SqlQueue: poll + drainTo(batchSize)
SqlQueue -> Repository: saveFunction.accept(batch)
Repository -> Pg: TransactionTemplate transaction
alt commit succeeds
  Pg --> Repository: commit
  Repository --> SqlQueue: return
  SqlQueue -> DaoCaller: future.set(null) for each entity
else transaction/save throws
  Pg --> Repository: rollback/error
  Repository --> SqlQueue: Throwable
  SqlQueue -> DaoCaller: future.setException(t) for each entity
  SqlQueue -> SqlQueue: clear batch; no automatic requeue
end

group PostgreSQL crash windows
  DaoCaller -> SqlQueue: add(entity)
  SqlQueue -x Repository: process dies before save; entity lost from JVM
  Repository -> Pg: commit another batch
  Pg --> Repository: success
  Repository -x SqlQueue: process dies before future.set; upstream may replay
end

== Cassandra buffered future ==
DaoCaller -> CassExec: submit(task)
alt tenant rate limit / queue full
  CassExec --> DaoCaller: future exception
else queued
  CassExec -> Driver: execute(task)
  alt execute / withTimeout / addCallback throws synchronously
    Driver --> CassExec: Throwable before callback installation completes
    CassExec -> CassExec: outer catch: failed++ / concurrency-- / debug log
    note over CassExec,DaoCaller
    taskCtx.future is not completed with setException.
    The caller future may remain pending forever.
    end note
  else callback installed
    Driver -> Cassandra: async request
    alt timeout or driver failure callback
      Driver --> CassExec: onFailure(t)
      CassExec --> DaoCaller: future.setException(t); no executor requeue
    else success callback
      Cassandra --> Driver: result
      Driver --> CassExec: onSuccess(result)
      CassExec --> DaoCaller: future.set(result)
    end
  end
end

== Observability fan-out and blind spots ==
SqlQueue -> Stats: successful/failed/total AtomicInteger
SqlQueue -> Logs: queueSize + batch totals; periodic reset
Manager -> Stats: RE tmp/final counters and optional timers
Stats -> Prom: Micrometer counters/gauges/timers
Lag -> Broker: committed offsets + end offsets
Lag -> Logs: non-zero lag at INFO
Actors -> Events: debug/error event when enabled and not limited
DaoCaller -> Events: audit action when enabled by mask

note over Stats,Prom
metrics.enabled=false stubs StatsCounter's Micrometer Counter,
but AtomicInteger log windows remain. Gauge/Timer registration follows their
own call paths. Prometheus also requires actuator endpoint exposure.
end note

note over Logs,Events
There is no common end-to-end trace, durable attempt ledger, Actor mailbox gauge,
application DLQ, SQL oldest-age metric, or unified provider async-ack failure metric.
Debug Event and Audit are fallible side channels, not completion receipts.
end note

@enduml
